  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This article is already attracting attention beyond its publication venue: one downstream reuse has been documented, and a Language Log post has highlighted the paper\u2019s challenge to the long-standing valence-arousal-dominance framework. The record is still early on the citation side, so the clearest evidence at present is this combination of scholarly reuse, open dissemination, and targeted commentary.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in <em>Science Advances</em> in 2026, this open-access article proposes a rethinking of the \u201cessence of meaning\u201d in language. Rather than treating valence, arousal, and dominance as the fundamental dimensions, the authors argue for a power-danger-structure framework, drawing on analyses of English words and tokens with automatically annotated \u201cousiograms.\u201d The paper sits at the intersection of linguistics, cognitive science, natural language processing, and AI, and it is backed by a multi-institution author team spanning the University of Vermont, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, The University of Adelaide, and the University of Massachusetts Amherst.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of impact, the strongest signal so far is reuse: Europe PMC has already recorded one downstream article mention associated with this work. That does not by itself establish broad scholarly influence, but it does show the paper is beginning to circulate within the literature. The article is also openly available in gold open access form and has associated Zenodo records and code/data stewardship signals, which support transparency and reuse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public discussion is modest but real. A June 2026 post on Language Log singled out the paper\u2019s central claim as a significant shift in the essence-of-meaning framework, indicating that the article has reached an audience interested in language, semantics, and the history of sentiment theory. At the same time, the broader attention record remains sparse: there are no OpenAlex citations yet, no scite-captured citations, and no captured event-data mentions in the available archive.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Taken together, the early footprint suggests a paper with conceptual ambition and clear relevance to sentiment analysis and meaning representation, but one whose longer-term influence is still unfolding. The record currently shows careful dissemination, documented reuse, and focused commentary rather than a mature citation trail.</p>",
